<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.20.xsd">

    <!--
      Repairs permission rows that the changelog declares and existing databases never received,
      and adds the one code that was enforced but declared nowhere.

      WHY THIS IS A REPAIR AND NOT A NEW DECLARATION.
      Changeset 030 ran on 2026-06-26 and was edited afterwards. It now declares
      pos.order.void.any and finance.expense.approve (with grants to MANAGER and ACCOUNTANT) that
      no database which already ran 030 will ever see — Liquibase only applies a changeset once.
      The changelog and the running system therefore disagree, and they disagree silently: nothing
      fails at startup, and the missing permissions surface only as a user being refused something
      they should be able to do.

      What that cost, observed on the dev stack:
        * OWNER and MANAGER saw ZERO orders in Order Management. OrderServiceImpl scopes the list to
          the caller's own orders unless they hold pos.order.view.all — a code declared nowhere at
          all — and every order belongs to the cashier who rang it up.
        * OWNER and MANAGER both got 403 voiding a cashier's order. pos.rego allows a void on
          pos.order.void.own or pos.order.void.any; neither role had .any, because 030's insert
          never ran.
        * No user on any tenant could approve an expense.

      The inserts below are idempotent (ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING) precisely because the two cases must
      converge: on a database that already ran the edited 030 they are a no-op, and on a database
      that ran the original 030 they supply what is missing. A plain <insert> would fail on the
      first and silently entrench the divergence on the second.

      pos.order.view.all is genuinely new — it is the only code here that no changeset ever declared.

      Note also that this database contains a `pos.order.void` row that NO changeset declares, held
      by OWNER/TENANT_ADMIN/MANAGER. It is a hand-applied row: the OPA policy never reads it, so it
      grants nothing. It is left in place rather than deleted (removing granted permissions is not a
      migration's job) and the controller gates now name the declared .own/.any codes instead.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-049-repair-permission-catalog-drift" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">

        <sql>
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('pos.order.view.all', 'pos', 'View all orders on the branch, not only your own'),
              ('pos.order.void.any', 'pos', 'Void any POS order (manager-level)'),
              ('pos.order.void.own', 'pos', 'Void own POS orders'),
              ('finance.expense.approve', 'finance', 'Approve expense requests')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'pos.order.view.all'),
              ('OWNER',        'pos.order.void.any'),
              ('OWNER',        'finance.expense.approve'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'pos.order.view.all'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'pos.order.void.any'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'finance.expense.approve'),
              ('MANAGER',      'pos.order.view.all'),
              ('MANAGER',      'pos.order.void.any'),
              ('MANAGER',      'pos.order.void.own'),
              ('MANAGER',      'finance.expense.approve'),
              ('ACCOUNTANT',   'finance.expense.approve')
            ON CONFLICT (role_code, permission_code) DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <!--
          CASHIER deliberately receives nothing here: it already holds pos.order.void.own from 043,
          and .view.all / .void.any are the supervisor codes it must not have.
        -->

    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
